{
  "requirement": "A bundled skill MAY put material needed only in specialized situations in files it links to. Each such link MUST say when to read the file. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** The only MUST is that each link from a bundled skill to a reference file says when to read the file. The MAY imposes nothing to check. Each skill upholds the MUST on its own, so it belongs to each site's review and needs no separate test here.\n\n**Site query `requirements.cli.skill_conventions.references/skills`.**\n- I ran it. It matches the front matter of exactly five files: `assets/skills/habeas/SKILL.md`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements/SKILL.md`, `habeas-evidence/SKILL.md`, `habeas-refactor/SKILL.md` and `habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`.\n- A repo-wide search for `^name: ` and `^description: Use` finds only those five files. The query misses no skill.\n- The reference files under `references/` have no front matter, so the query does not select them. That fits the text, which says a site is a skill \"recognized by its front matter\".\n- The query is limited to `assets/skills`, which holds all the bundled skills, so nothing outside the scope is selected.\n- Its snippets fit the rule. The match snippet has `---\\nname: example\\n---` front matter. The no_match snippet is a plain `# Example` heading with no front matter, which is the nearby non-skill markdown the rule must not select.\n- The requirement has no violation queries, so there is nothing to assess there.\n\n**Test `bundled_skills_say_when_to_read_each_reference_file` in `tests/authoring_cli.rs`.** It is a supporting test and not required for adequacy. It still checks the obligation well:\n- It lists the expected guidance sentence for each link in the `links` and `reference_guidance` tables.\n- It asserts that each sentence is present in the skill text.\n- It uses a regex to find every `references/*.md` pointer in every skill file and asserts that each pointer sits inside one of those checked sentences.\n- It asserts that every file in a skill's directory is linked.\n\nNo gap found.",
  "references": "5b5a1f7cf171bc78f06adc8576f6e3e3dcb20c5f062af5c778e2b34a76dbfa84",
